# Phase 5 (PROBE_SELFHOST.md) — function representation.
#
# Established separately (transcripts/phase5_absence.txt): functions are NOT
# first-class values. `r = { f: double }` raises wrong-arity because a bare
# function name is a zero-arg CALL, not a reference; `r.f of 3` is a syntax
# error because a call names a literal function, never an expression. A
# function cannot be stored in a record, passed as a value, or called
# indirectly.
#
# The real answer, and how interp.py already works (its Function dataclass is
# params + body + captured env): represent a user function as INERT DATA — a
# record carrying a parameter name, a body AST, and a captured environment —
# and APPLY it by interpretation, never by host-calling. This program is that
# representation, in Planes, end to end. It proves the three things the phase
# asks for: a closure can be stored in a record and retrieved; it closes over
# its defining scope; and it is applied by walking its body.

# env is the association idiom from Phase 1: a list of { name, value }.
to lookup of env, key:
  result = nothing
  for each entry in env where entry.name == key:
    result = entry.value
  give result

# eval one expression-AST node under an env. Recursive over sub-expressions,
# dispatched by shape (Phase 2). This is the interpreter core, in miniature.
to eval-expr of node, env:
  result = nothing
  when node is { kind: "lit", value }:
    result = value
  else:
    when node is { kind: "var", name }:
      result = lookup of env, name
    else:
      when node is { kind: "add", left, right }:
        result = (eval-expr of left, env) + (eval-expr of right, env)
      else:
        result = nothing
  give result

# Apply a closure (inert data) to an argument: bind the parameter on top of
# the CAPTURED environment, then interpret the body. No host call anywhere.
to apply-closure of closure, arg:
  call-env = closure.env plus { name: closure.param, value: arg }
  give eval-expr of closure.body, call-env

# make-adder returns a closure that adds a captured n to its argument x.
# The body is `x + n` as AST; the captured env carries n. Two calls with
# different n must produce two closures that remember different n's.
to make-adder of n:
  xref = { kind: "var", name: "x" }
  nref = { kind: "var", name: "n" }
  body = { kind: "add", left: xref, right: nref }
  captured = [{ name: "n", value: n }]
  give { param: "x", body: body, env: captured }

add5 = make-adder of 5

# Store the closure in a record, retrieve it, apply it — proving a closure
# survives being stored and retrieved (question 2), which the direct
# function-value path forbids.
holder = { fn: add5 }
retrieved = holder.fn
show "add5 applied to 10 (via a record field):"
show apply-closure of retrieved, 10

# Distinct captures prove it closes over its defining scope (question 2).
add20 = make-adder of 20
show "add5 to 100, then add20 to 3 — distinct captured n:"
show apply-closure of add5, 100
show apply-closure of add20, 3
